.TH OBSIDIAN 1 "November 2024" "obsidian 0.1.0" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
obsidian \- policy-driven Debian security and privacy toolkit
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B obsidian
[\fIglobal flags\fR] \fIcommand\fR [\fIcommand flags\fR]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B obsidian
manages firewall rules, SSH hardening, kernel sysctls, audit rules, log
retention, unattended upgrades, and service sandboxing from a single
declarative TOML policy. All operations are transactional, reversible, and
designed to never accidentally lock you out of a remote server.
.SH COMMANDS
.TP
.B init
Write a starter policy to \fI/etc/obsidian/policy.toml\fR.
.TP
.B check
Parse and validate the policy, showing warnings.
.TP
.B plan
Show every change \fBapply\fR would make, with diffs.
.TP
.B apply
Apply the policy transactionally: snapshot \fB\->\fR apply \fB\->\fR verify
\fB\->\fR commit. Rolls back automatically on any failure.
.TP
.B verify
Compare the live host to the policy; exit 1 on drift.
.TP
.B drift
Like \fBverify\fR but show only drift items.
.TP
.B status
Show the applied policy digest, managed files, services, and recent
transactions.
.TP
.B rollback [\fB--transaction\fR \fIID\fR] [\fB--list\fR]
Restore a previous transaction. Without \fB--transaction\fR, rolls back the
newest committed transaction. Use \fB--list\fR to see available transactions.
.TP
.B isolate \fINAME\fR
Apply only the sandbox named \fINAME\fR (equivalent to \fBapply --sandbox
NAME\fR).
.TP
.B render [\fB--output\fR \fIDIR\fR]
Render all managed files to stdout or to \fIDIR\fR without touching the host.
.TP
.B explain
Print a human-readable explanation of what the policy asks for and why.
.TP
.B version
Print the version and exit.
.TP
.B help
Show this help message.
.SH GLOBAL FLAGS
.TP
.B --config \fIPATH\fR
Policy file (default \fI/etc/obsidian/policy.toml\fR).
.TP
.B --json
Machine-readable JSON output.
.TP
.B --no-color
Disable colored output.
.TP
.B --verbose
Show every command and check.
.TP
.B --strict
Treat policy warnings as errors.
.TP
.B --yes
Do not ask for confirmation (non-interactive).
.TP
.B --dry-run
Plan only, never change the host.
.TP
.B --allow-lockout
Permit changes that may end the current SSH session.
.TP
.B --allow-degraded
Continue when a requested isolation feature (AppArmor, Landlock, seccomp)
is unavailable on the host.
.TP
.B --no-packages
Never install packages via apt.
.TP
.B --sandbox \fINAME\fR
Limit plan/apply/verify to the sandbox named \fINAME\fR.
.TP
.B --timeout \fISECONDS\fR
Watchdog grace period (default 120).
.SH POLICY FILE
The policy is a TOML file with the following keys:
.TP
.B role
One of \fIweb-server\fR, \fIssh-bastion\fR, \fIworkstation\fR, \fIcustom\fR.
.TP
.B public_interface
The network interface that carries public traffic (e.g. \fIeth0\fR).
.TP
.B [exposure]
Network exposure modes for each service: \fIdisabled\fR, \fIloopback\fR,
\fIadmin-only\fR, or \fIpublic\fR. Keys: \fIssh\fR, \fIhttps\fR, \fIhttp\fR,
\fIsmtp\fR, \fIdns\fR, \fIntp\fR.
.TP
.B [admin]
Administrative access settings: \fIsource_cidrs\fR (required for admin-only
exposure), \fIinterfaces\fR, \fIssh_port\fR, \fIpassword_auth\fR,
\fIroot_login\fR (\fIno\fR, \fIprohibit-password\fR, \fIyes\fR),
\fIallow_tcp_forwarding\fR.
.TP
.B [privacy]
\fItelemetry\fR (\fIdeny\fR/\fIallow\fR), \fIdns\fR (\fIsystem\fR/\fIlocal-resolver\fR),
\fIdns_servers\fR, \fIdns_over_tls\fR, \fIlog_retention_days\fR, \fIlog_max_mb\fR.
.TP
.B [services]
Map of systemd unit name to desired state: \fIenabled\fR, \fIdisabled\fR,
\fImasked\fR.
.TP
.B [sandbox.\fINAME\fR]
Service isolation: \fIbinary\fR, \fIrun_as\fR, \fIgroup\fR, \fIargs\fR,
\fIread_write\fR, \fIread_only\fR, \fInetwork\fR (\fInone\fR, \fIloopback\fR,
\fIoutbound-https\fR, \fIoutbound\fR), \fIapparmor\fR, \fIlandlock\fR,
\fIrestart\fR, \fIsyscall_filter\fR.
.TP
.B [updates]
\fIunattended\fR, \fIauto_reboot\fR, \fIreboot_time\fR.
.TP
.B [audit]
\fIenabled\fR.
.TP
.B [hardening]
\fIsysctl\fR, \fIssh\fR.
.SH SAFETY
If \fBobsidian\fR detects it is running inside an SSH session (via
\fISSH_CONNECTION\fR, \fISSH_CLIENT\fR, or process ancestry), it refuses to
apply firewall or SSH changes that would drop that session, unless
\fB--allow-lockout\fR is given. This prevents accidental lockout of remote
servers.
.SH TRANSACTIONS
Every \fBapply\fR creates a transaction directory under
\fI/var/lib/obsidian/transactions/\fR containing snapshots of all managed
files, service states, and nftables rulesets. On failure, the transaction is
rolled back automatically. Use \fBrollback\fR to restore a previous state
manually.
.SH FILES
.TP
\fI/etc/obsidian/policy.toml\fR
Policy file.
.TP
\fI/var/lib/obsidian/state.json\fR
Current applied state.
.TP
\fI/var/lib/obsidian/transactions/\fR
Transaction snapshots.
.TP
\fI/run/obsidian.lock\fR
Global lock preventing concurrent applies.
.SH EXAMPLES
.TP
.B Create and edit a policy
.in +4
.nf
$ sudo obsidian init
$ sudo vim /etc/obsidian/policy.toml
.fi
.in
.TP
.B Preview and apply
.in +4
.nf
$ sudo obsidian plan
$ sudo obsidian apply
.fi
.in
.TP
.B Verify and show drift
.in +4
.nf
$ sudo obsidian verify
$ sudo obsidian drift
.fi
.in
.TP
.B Roll back the last apply
.in +4
.nf
$ sudo obsidian rollback
.fi
.in
.TP
.B Apply only one sandbox
.in +4
.nf
$ sudo obsidian isolate myapp
.fi
.in
